WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.430
From an action-packed sci-fi adventure to
a tweaked modern take on a classic sitcom,

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.080
these are the the best TV shows of 2018.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.370
Throw some popcorn in the microwave and dig
out your binge-watching sweatpants, because

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.360
we're diving in.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.140
Who would've guessed that it would take a
cartoon about a foul-mouthed, anthropomorphic

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:22.580
horse to really nail the trials and tribulations
of fame, depression, and generational trauma?

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:28.270
Basically, BoJack Horseman has been a revelation,
balancing wit, humor, and plenty of madcap

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:32.720
charm with a deep emphasis on the way sadness
permeates… well, everything.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.080
And, even for those familiar with how brutally
tragic and unflinching the show can get, season

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.140
5 took BoJack Horseman to new heights.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:41.740
"You're here.

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:43.440
And I hate you.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.020
But you're my best friend.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:47.980
And you need me."

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.000
Plenty of other shows have featured anti-hero
leads, but BoJack never loses sight of the

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.190
fact that its titular lead's crappier actions
remain forever present, and the weight of

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.840
his awful actions are hiding just below the
surface.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.560
Season 5 brought all that subtext directly
to the forefront as BoJack struggled to escape

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.780
his past mistakes, even when confronting them
might be the only way he can ever actually

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:09.440
be free.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.660
And, beyond the weighty subject matter, season
5 was an ambitious exercise from a show that

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.320
thrives on pushing the envelope.

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.320
For instance, who would've guessed that one
of the season's highlights would be a 20-minute

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.970
monologue by BoJack, performed with a hypnotizing
bittersweetness by actor Will Arnett.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.799
BoJack has always been one of the best shows
on Netflix, but season 5 cemented the show

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.300
as one of the best series on television, period.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:37.020
There have been plenty of movies and television
shows about acting and Hollywood life, but

00:01:37.021 --> 00:01:41.560
no show has managed to capture the unique
strangeness of struggling actors in Los Angeles

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:42.340
quite like Barry.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.620
The show follows Bill Hader as Barry, a hitman
who's struggling with a growing sense of ennui

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:51.549
and finds his life changed when he stumbles
into an acting class and gets bitten by the

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:52.480
acting bug.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.320
"These are professional actors, and they're
the real deal.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.620
And they say I got something."

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:02.180
What follows is a hilariously dark comedy,
as Barry struggles to leave his life of crime

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:07.200
behind to make it as an actor even while former
clients and a manipulative mentor conspire

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.500
to keep him in their clutches.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:17.760
"If you kill him, anything, I'll come back
here, and I'll kill every single one of you."

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.160
Through it all, Barry is equally funny and
ruthless about what it means to make it in

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.280
the City of Angels.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.280
Barry actually is a nice guy, but he's also
a reluctant contract killer.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:31.420
Really, he just wants to follow his dreams,
like many a young actor in Hollywood.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.500
Anyone who's ever attended a friend's improv
class or been asked to look over a movie script

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.180
will find plenty to relate to in Barry.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.000
After coming out with a strong start and a
lot of positive reviews, the Marvel Netflix

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.220
shows have faltered somewhat over the years.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.300
Iron Fist seemed like a misfire almost immediately,
and a better-reviewed second season didn't

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:54.460
manage to keep it from getting canceled, a
fate that was also suffered by Luke Cage.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.700
With all this dour news, it would have been
easy for Daredevil season 3 to come and go

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.400
as silently as the ninjas Matt Murdock fights.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.930
But, as it turns out, this batch of episodes
came out swinging, and the new season is a

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.640
perfect reminder of why people were excited
about the Netflix Marvel shows to begin with.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.739
Charlie Cox returns as Matt Murdock, bringing
all of his considerable angst and moody charisma

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:16.500
to the fore.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.080
Cox has a lot to work with, too; season 3
puts Daredevil in just about the worst position

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:26.080
imaginable, grinding the hero down to paste
just to see if he can build himself back up.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.700
It's a thrilling season with jaw-dropping
action and some of the best acting on television.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.900
Best of all, Vincent D'Onofrio returns as
Wilson Fisk, the charismatic Kingpin whose

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.140
brutality is only matched by his skill at
manipulation.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.760
"I have made many mistakes.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.180
But I accept the debt I'm paying because of
them."

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:54.020
Despite Daredevil's success, on November 29th,
Netflix announced the show would be cancelled.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.620
Here's to hoping Netflix's canceled Marvel
shows continue their heroics on some other

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.960
platform in the future.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.820
After returning to television for the first
time in twenty years in 2005, Doctor Who re-established

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:09.080
itself as a stylish sci-fi show for a new
audience.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.400
Still, despite multiple showrunners and multiple
actors playing the eponymous Doctor, the next

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.540
decade of Doctor Who settled into a comfortable
rhythm: an eccentric male lead, on-again off-again

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.790
romances with various co-stars, callbacks
to the original run, and silly science-fiction

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:25.560
shenanigans.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.200
"No bo ho sho fro ko ro to sho.

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.700
Posfokotopobajo."

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:38.880
That all changed in 2018 when, for the first
time, a woman took over as the title character.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.030
Jodie Whitaker's turn as the Doctor ended
up being exactly the shot in the arm that

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.380
the show needed, coinciding nicely with new
showrunner Chris Chibnall's tenure.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.720
Based on her web series Awkward Black Girl,
Insecure follows a fictionalized version of

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.910
creator and star Issa Rae as she grapples
with the complexities of black life, feminism,

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.400
and millennial culture.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:04.860
Insecure is whip-smart about portraying complicated
issues with nuance and genuine humor.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.060
It's easily one of the funniest shows on television,
and packed to the brim with wit and emotion.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.140
Season three hasn't just kept up the show's
hot streak, either; it's arguably the best

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:14.640
yet.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:19.600
Michael Andor Brodeur at Boston Globe writes
that "Insecure has never felt more confident,"

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.820
and it's hard to disagree.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.080
Even three seasons deep into the show, Rae's
abilities as a writer, showrunner, producer,

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:29.000
and performer have never been so obviously
apparent.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:34.480
Insecure keeps growing, improving, and changing
like few other shows, and season three was

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.880
a perfect reminder that you should be making
it appointment television.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.210
The Haunting of Hill House is horror at its
best, simultaneously terrifying and thrilling

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:47.240
while also dealing with the emotional core
that should be at the center of every horror

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:47.740
movie.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.580
That richness of character has led to some
reviews calling Hill House "a drama with ghosts"

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:56.770
or "like This Is Us wrapped in a gothic ghost
story," but make no mistake.

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:58.760
Hill House is absolutely terrifying.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.680
Director/showrunner Mike Flanagan showcases
all the technical brilliance that made Oculus

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.410
and Gerald's Game so profoundly scary, and
through it all, he never loses sight of the

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:12.550
fact that the ghosts are a way for the audience
to connect to the Crain family's trauma.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.400
It's just that, y'know, they're also scary
as hell.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:20.580
Fans of classic '90s sitcoms rejoiced when
ABC announced that they'd greenlit a revival

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.639
of Roseanne; for many viewers, the show had
earned its place in history alongside Cheers,

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:28.260
Frasier, and other classic television comedies.

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.940
Unfortunately, the jubilation didn't last
long.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:35.260
Just a couple months after the new series
premiered, star Roseanne Barr tweeted out

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:39.300
a racist statement that almost immediately
led to the show's cancellation.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.090
Luckily, even though it shared a name with
its star, Roseanne was never just about Roseanne.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:49.220
The creative talent behind the show quickly
pivoted to making The Conners, a continuation

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:53.380
of the fans' favorite family, minus Roseanne,
of course, whose character had died off-screen.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.460
"What about when the casseroles run out?

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:57.880
What if the person who died is the only one
who cooked?"

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.520
"Then we keep telling people we're grieving
until we figure out how to feed ourselves."

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.110
Critical reception to the newly tweaked show
has been largely positive, with critics like

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.590
Alan Sepinwall of Rolling Stone writing:

00:07:08.590 --> 00:07:12.840
"There are enough sturdy characters and performances
here to move on without her, and the premiere

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.800
felt more focused than many of the episodes
from the spring."

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.600
Donald Glover and his team had a lot to live
up to when the second season of Atlanta was

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:25.039
announced: the show's first season firmly
established it as one of the most electrifying

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:27.190
television series in recent memory.

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:32.110
And Glover's performance as Earn in season
two of Atlanta, subtitled "Robbin' Season,"

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:36.640
is as good as you'd expect, but it also showcases
other characters with a confidence that you

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.380
won't see on any other show.

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.300
"I'm just gonna take this s---."

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.260
"Wait, what?"

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:44.980
"Hell yeah.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.700
They got a 'no chase' policy."

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:51.949
The season was filled with standout episodes
highlighting the other characters in the show,

00:07:51.949 --> 00:07:56.889
like the nightmarish "Teddy Perkins," which
followed Darius, played by Lakeith Stanfield,

00:07:56.889 --> 00:08:00.160
meeting a Michael Jackson-esque musician in
an ominous mansion.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:03.860
"Woods," on the other hand, followed Brian
Tyree Henry's Al, A.K.A.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:07.860
"Paper Boi," as he's haunted by the downsides
of his own fame and success.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.760
More than half of the season was directed
by Hiro Murai, Glover's longtime music video

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.599
director, and Murai's expert visual sense
and rich use of surrealism has made Atlanta

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:19.660
one of the best-looking shows on television.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.060
As the prequel, and sometimes-knda-sequel
to Breaking Bad, Better Call Saul takes a

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:28.330
bit of a slower pace than its predecessor,
but is still prone to occasional explosions

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.020
of violence.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:33.120
You're more likely to see elaborate cons than
the vicious meth heads that populated Breaking

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.480
Bad, but the underlying moral decay and fantastic
character writing is even better.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:41.940
Bob Odenkirk fills Jimmy with such warmth
and humanity that it's like watching a horror

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.980
movie as he slowly sinks into corruption,
losing his last bits of morality in the process.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:51.700
Even for a show as consistent as Better Call
Saul, season four was a victory lap that took

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:56.290
viewers closer to the events of Breaking Bad
than anyone really expected, and we couldn't

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:57.900
be more excited for what's to come.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.460
Much like a Greek tragedy, we already know
that Jimmy is destined for moral rot and a

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.770
job managing a Cinnabon (Which sounds awesome!

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.641
How can you not like Cinnabon?), the beauty
of the show is how much you don't want it

00:09:08.641 --> 00:09:09.200
to happen.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.360
DC's new take on a familiar cast of caped
crusaders, Titans needed just two glorious

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.140
words to get fans riled up:

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:17.700
"F--- Batman."

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:22.220
Sure, Robin and the rest of the titular team
look like they just bought out a Hot Topic,

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:25.830
but critics dig DC's gritty reimagining of
the young super heroes.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:30.589
For instance, Autumn Noel Kelly at Newsweek
wrote positively about the show, claiming:

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:33.520
"Titans is the most balanced superhero show
out there.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.050
It's skillfully constructed, narratively speaking,
and there's a surprising amount of flavor

00:09:38.050 --> 00:09:41.600
beneath its classification as a dark, moody,
teen drama."

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:45.700
Turns out, there's more pleasure to be had
in Titans than watching Robin drop the f-bomb!

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.640
HBO's True Detective brought Cary Fukunaga's
work to a mainstream audience.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.580
His keen eye for cinematography was a key
component of the show's season one success.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:00.300
After that, he released Beasts of No Nation,
which was met with similarly positive critical

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:00.800
acclaim.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:05.040
Then, he more or less disappeared for three
years before blazing back to the small screen

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:05.860
with Maniac.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:07.080
The wait was worth it.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.480
Starring Jonah Hill and Emma Stone as patients
in an experimental mental health study in

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:16.800
a retro-futuristic dystopia, the beautifully
shot Maniac jumps from genre to genre through

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.360
Owen and Annie's fantasies.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:23.220
And Fukunaga keeps the focus squarely on the
difficulty of living with mental illness and

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:28.700
trauma and the possibility of finding connection
and community despite, or even because of,

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:29.200
those issues.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:32.060
"Of course you make me happy.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:36.860
You always made me happy."

